  receipts: [
    "THE FAILURE MODE: the resolver's ambient primitive-authority tier (v2.compiler.resolve resolve_atom_primitive_authority) binds an unimported spelling -- first, contains, map_get, starts_with, join, split, replace, string_length, fold, keys, values -- by looking up a contract row (std.primitives.<name>_contract) in the symbol index of the modules the run LOADED. While the scoped census loaded exactly the named subjects' closures, whether such a name bound was a fact about the invocation's subject list, not about the module: the same module, same binary, emitted binding_partial ... ambient:primitive_authority std.primitives.first_contract in one run and binding_unbound in another, because std.primitives rode the first run's closures and not the second's. A census consumer comparing base against shards then reads the difference as a behavior change of the module and refuses a merge that changed nothing -- which is exactly how this blocked //gunbc/instruments:namespace-wave-binding-diff's positive merge for #14324.",

    "THE TELL: a binding row for a module whose bytes are byte-identical between two runs differs in binding state (partial<->unbound), the flipped names are all ambient primitive spellings, and the two runs loaded different module populations (check the census_run_comparable population field, or the run's subject list). The module's own imports are unchanged and do not name the substrate -- ambient resolution is import-free by design, which is what makes the population an invisible dependency.",

    "EXECUTED REPRODUCTION, one binary (emitted driver, sha256 2742801a..., head 3cd5c856e48f, instrument route //gunbc/instruments:v2-native-census-scoped), the subject list the ONLY variable: census-scoped //std/fermi:all prints 'binding_unbound std.fermi first 72:20' while census-scoped //std/fermi:all //std/primitives:all prints 'binding std.fermi first 0 ambient:primitive_authority std.primitives.first_contract 72:20'; the uri_path pair (//extdeps/uri_path:all plus/minus //std/primitives:all) shows the same flip on 'contains' at 82:15, aggregate binding_unbound 157 vs 58. THE FLEET ROWS (clever-carp-254, fermi/ at scratch branch 359d18ef): base tree 02b9486ad13 vs head 80fe982d7f37, std.fermi byte-identical, same instrument revision acb862a78f625330-d48b9c56c12811d2 -- exactly a 3-row delta (binding_partial first ... first_contract 72:20 + its two module-count rollups, versus binding_unbound) across base (1,948-module population) and all four shards (165 modules); the four shards agree with each other as sets. sunny-bee-153's EVIDENCE.txt: std.primitives has 0 rows in the shard population vs 374 at base, and every untouched-module difference is one of ~25 primitive spellings flipping partial->unbound.",

    "THE EARLIEST BOUNDARY (DESIGN 6b): not the resolver tier -- reading the loaded index is what a namespace lookup IS. The boundary is the ACQUISITION: native_lane_census_scoped_ingest (v2.compiler.source_root_eval) asked for the subjects' closures and nothing else, so whether the tier's own contract module was in the namespace depended on an import path nobody declared. The fix sits there: native_census_scoped_entries appends the declared substrate roster -- read from the authority's OWN declaration (v2.std.primitive_contract_ref primitive_contract_module_path), so roster and tier cannot drift -- once, at the one shared ingest every census and wave spawn passes through. The RED that keeps this honest is executed, not asserted: the same module's rows must be identical across two subject populations on one binary (they are after the fix: fermi totals 2254 partial / 48 unbound both ways), with the without-arm flip above enrolled as the regression control, and three claims in v2.test.claim.census_scoped_acquisition pin the fold (substrate rides every population; never duplicated; subjects never dropped or reordered).",

    "THE OPEN HALF, recorded here so it is not lost: the fleet ALSO reported per-module observation flips (gunbc.host_names.gen_z and extdeps.bmc.ipmi_boot_selection reading ObservationComplete in some shard ranges and ObservationIncomplete with reason native_census_infer_stops_at_first_fatal in others, same binary). That pairing does not exist at main: the reason is minted only in the InferRefused arm of native_census_infer_completeness and cannot pair with outcome=resolve_refused, and a bisect over the shard-B subject prefix (223, 112, 56, 28, 14, 7, 3, 1 subjects, and gen_z alone) shows the printed observation never flips on any population -- so the report is an unverified transcription (its author's own caution, relayed by deep-hawk-258) or a reading of the cause-group STAGE rather than the observation field. A second population-read candidate -- the emitted walk threading context = step.context.clone() across modules (src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag) so module N resolves against module N-1's published closure -- is real as code but produced no observation flip in the bisect. NEITHER is claimed as fixed. The next rung: a fleet re-run on the fixed identity (base + shards + positive merge/4) either reproduces an observation flip -- at which point the per-module context threading becomes the suspect and its own trigger fires -- or retires the report; a per-round substrate re-ingest measured by clever-carp-254 (22 clusters x ~5m07s in the binding-diff walk, population-independent) is the same open half's cost face and stays open until the round count in a fleet log drops to one substrate ingest.",

